reference_steps:
We draw a regular hexagon and one of its longest diagonals: [asy]
size(80);
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1.5,.5*sqrt(3))--(1,sqrt(3))--(0,sqrt(3))--(-.5,.5*sqrt(3))--cycle);
draw((1,0)--(0,sqrt(3)));
[/asy] Now we draw in the other two long diagonals.  We have split the hexagon into six equilateral triangles, which are congruent by symmetry.  [asy]
size(80);
draw((0,0)--(1,0)--(1.5,.5*sqrt(3))--(1,sqrt(3))--(0,sqrt(3))--(-.5,.5*sqrt(3))--cycle);
draw((1,0)--(0,sqrt(3))); draw((1.5,.5*sqrt(3))--(-.5,.5*sqrt(3))); draw((1,sqrt(3))--(0,0));
[/asy] We see that two side lengths make up one long diagonal.  Let the hexagon's side length be $s$, then, its long diagonal has length $2s$ and its perimeter has length $6s$.  The ratio of its long diagonal to its perimeter is hence $\frac{2s}{6s}=\boxed{\frac{1}{3}}$.

problem:
The figure below shows a grid of $10$ squares in a row. Each square has a diagonal connecting its lower left vertex to its upper right vertex. A bug moves along the line segments from vertex to vertex, never traversing the same segment twice and never moving from right to left along a horizontal or diagonal segment. Let $N$ be the number of paths the bug can take from the lower left corner ($A$) to the upper right corner ($B$). One such path from $A$ to $B$ is shown by the thick line segments in the figure. Find $\sqrt{N}$.

[asy]
size(12cm);
draw((0, 0)--(0, 1));
for (int i = 0; i <= 9; i += 1)
{
draw((i, 1)--(i + 1, 1)--(i + 1, 0)--(i, 0)--(i + 1, 1));
}
draw((0, 0)--(0, 1)--(2, 1)--(2, 0)--(3, 1)--(3, 0)--(4, 1)--(4, 0)--(5, 0)--(5, 1)--(6, 1)--(6, 0)--(7, 1)--(8, 1)--(8, 0)--(10, 0)--(10, 1), red + linewidth(2));
